"""Printed55–57 exercise with separately authored learning answers."""
from html import escape
MCQS=[]
def mcq(n,q,opts,ans,why,page=55):
 MCQS.append(dict(qNo=n,q=q,opts=opts,ans=ans,explanation=why,sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=page)],solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer'))
mcq('i','Shifts in water-solute balance are managed primarily by',['respiratory system','the urinary system','endocrine adjustments','the circulatory system'],1,'The urinary system adjusts water and solute excretion. Hormones help regulate this kidney response.')
mcq('ii','Which is the most important mechanism for water loss from the body?',['excretion in urine','sneezing','sweating','elimination in feces'],0,'Under ordinary conditions, urine is the major regulated route of water loss described in this chapter.')
mcq('iii','The process that normally exerts the greatest control over the water balance of an individual is',['sweating','kidney function.','evaporation through the skin.','respiratory loss.'],1,'Kidneys vary the amount of water reabsorbed and the volume and concentration of urine.')
mcq('iv','Which of the following does NOT dispose off a type of waste directly to the environment?',['digestive system','respiratory system','circulatory system','urinary system'],2,'The circulatory system transports wastes to organs that release them outside the body.')
mcq('v','The most toxic substances routinely found in the blood are metabolites of what type of molecules?',['proteins','carbohydrates','nucleic acids','Fats'],0,'The intended answer is proteins: amino-group removal produces toxic ammonia. The lesson also identifies nucleic-acid metabolism as a source of nitrogenous waste, so the wording is broader than this single-choice answer.')
mcq('vi','Which of the following is the last structure that urine passes through during its excretion from body?',['distal tubule','urethra','urinary bladder','ureter'],1,'Urine leaves the bladder through the urethra to reach the exterior.')
mcq('vii','The process during which potassium and hydrogen ions and some toxic substances are put into urine is called',['tubular secretion.','reabsorption.','filtration.','countercurrent multiplication.'],0,'Tubular secretion transfers these substances from blood around the tubules into the tubular fluid.')
mcq('viii','Kidney health is described in terms of',['the number of kidney stones.','rate of filtration.','water retention.','blood clot'],1,'Filtration rate describes an important aspect of kidney function; it is the relevant measure among the listed options.',56)
mcq('ix','Why is there no glucose present in the filtrate in the distal tubule of a nephron?',['its molecules are too large to pass across the basement membrane','it is removed by osmosis from the tubule','it is passively absorb by the cells lining the descending the loop of Henle','it is actively absorb by the proximal tubule cells'],3,'Under normal conditions, filtered glucose is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule before the fluid reaches the distal tubule.',56)
mcq('x','In case of overheating, the body temperature is regulated by:',['more sweating and more urination','more sweating and less urination','less sweating and more urination','less sweating and less urination'],1,'The intended response combines greater evaporative cooling through sweating with conservation of water by reducing urine output.',56)
mcq('xi','An animal that warms itself mainly by absorbing heat from its surroundings is known as:',['homoiotherm','ectotherm','endotherm','none of them.'],1,'An ectotherm relies mainly on environmental heat to raise its body temperature.',56)

def solved(n,q,steps,answer,page=57):
 html='<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol>'
 for i,step in enumerate(steps):html+=f'<li data-example-step="{i}" style="'+('display:none;' if i else '')+'">'+escape(step)+'</li>'
 html+='</ol><button type="button" data-revealed="1" onclick="revealMathExampleStep(this)">Next step</button> <button type="button" onclick="resetMathExampleSteps(this)">Start again</button></div>'
 html+='<div data-example-answer style="display:none;margin-top:.7rem;"><strong>Answer: </strong>'+escape(answer)+'</div>'
 return dict(qNo=n,q=q,steps=steps,answer=html,plainAnswer=answer,solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=page)])
SHORT=[
solved('i','Why it is necessary for a living being to maintain its internal environment at a fairly constant level?',['Cells need suitable temperature, water content, ion concentration and pH.','Large changes disturb enzyme activity and metabolic reactions.','Homeostasis corrects deviations so tissues continue functioning despite external changes.'],'A stable internal environment keeps cells and enzymes working within suitable conditions and supports normal health and function.',56),
solved('ii','How positive feedback can be harmful at times?',['Positive feedback strengthens the original change.','If a harmful change is amplified without a stopping point, the system moves farther from a stable condition.','The chapter gives rising fever and breathing in air with a very high carbon-dioxide content as examples.'],'Positive feedback can amplify an abnormal change, causing progressively greater disturbance instead of restoring balance.',56),
solved('iii','Describe mechanism of ultrafiltration.',['Blood enters the glomerulus through the afferent arteriole.','The pressure in glomerular capillaries drives fluid across the filtration barrier into Bowman’s capsule.','Water and small dissolved substances enter the filtrate; large proteins and blood cells are normally retained in blood.','The resulting fluid is glomerular filtrate, which enters the renal tubule.'],'Ultrafiltration is pressure-driven movement of water and small solutes from glomerular blood into Bowman’s capsule.',56),
solved('iv','How regulation of blood flow to skin is meaningful to maintain body temperature?',['When hot, skin arterioles dilate, bringing more blood near the body surface.','This increases heat transfer from the skin to the surroundings.','When cold, skin arterioles constrict, reducing surface blood flow and conserving heat.'],'Vasodilation increases heat loss, whereas vasoconstriction reduces heat loss.',56),
solved('v','Why Juxtamedullary nephrons are more important in osmoregulation?',['Their long loops of Henle extend deep into the medulla.','The loops contribute to the medullary osmotic gradient through countercurrent processes.','This gradient supports water reabsorption and concentrated urine when water is scarce.'],'Their long loops create and use a deep medullary gradient that enables effective water conservation.'),
solved('vi','How ADH controls concentration of urine?',['Water shortage stimulates ADH release from the posterior pituitary.','ADH increases the water permeability of collecting ducts and the relevant distal nephron regions.','More water returns to the blood, so urine volume falls and concentration rises.','When ADH release is low, less water is reabsorbed and more dilute urine is produced.'],'More ADH produces less, more concentrated urine; less ADH produces more dilute urine.'),
solved('vii','Why do marine fishes drink water unlike fresh water fishes?',['The chapter’s comparison concerns marine bony fishes, whose body fluids are less concentrated than seawater.','They lose water osmotically and drink seawater to replace it, while removing excess salts.','Freshwater fishes gain water osmotically, so they excrete abundant dilute urine and have little need to drink.'],'Marine bony fishes drink to replace osmotic water loss; freshwater fishes must remove the water entering their bodies.'),
solved('viii','How do some terrestrial mammals become so resistant that they are able to survive without drinking water?',['Desert mammals obtain water from food and from oxidation of nutrients.','Nocturnal activity helps them avoid daytime heat and reduce water loss.','Efficient water conservation reduces the amount that must be replaced.','The chapter uses the kangaroo rat as its example.'],'Metabolic water, suitable food, heat-avoiding behaviour and effective water conservation can meet their water needs without drinking.'),
solved('ix','What is the benefit of Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL) over Per Cutaneous Nephro Lithotrypsy (PCNL).',['ESWL applies shock waves from outside the body to break suitable stones.','PCNL requires a tract through the back into the kidney to reach and remove stones.','Thus ESWL avoids that direct access tract for appropriate stones; the chapter notes that larger stones may require PCNL.'],'For suitable stones, ESWL is less invasive because it breaks stones without making the kidney-access tract required by PCNL.'),
solved('x','Why is the excretion of uric acid advantageous to birds and reptiles?',['Uric acid is poorly soluble and can be eliminated with little water.','This conserves water in terrestrial animals.','In shelled eggs, precipitated uric acid can remain as solid waste while the embryo develops.'],'Uric acid conserves water and permits nitrogenous waste to accumulate as a relatively insoluble solid within shelled eggs.'),
]
LONG=[
solved('i','Explain the working of feedback system in a living body and compare it with a non-living physical feedback system.',['Name the three components: a receptor detects change, a control centre compares information with a set point, and an effector produces a response.','In body-temperature control, thermoreceptors report temperature and the hypothalamus coordinates effectors such as sweat glands and muscles.','When hot, cooling responses reduce temperature; when cold, conservation and heat production raise it. This negative feedback opposes the original change.','In an incubator, a thermometer detects temperature, a thermostat compares it with the setting, and a heating coil supplies heat when needed.','Both systems use detection, comparison, response and feedback. Living systems use biological receptors, nerves, hormones and tissues; the physical system uses instruments and a heater.','Positive feedback instead amplifies a change, as in the chapter’s childbirth example, until the process reaches an endpoint.'],'Both biological and physical feedback systems detect deviations and activate effectors. Negative feedback restores a set point; positive feedback amplifies a change.'),
solved('ii','Describe different parts of urinary system of human with main emphasis on their role in excretion.',['Kidneys contain nephrons that filter blood, reabsorb useful substances and water, and secrete selected wastes into the tubules.','The collecting ducts deliver urine to the renal pelvis.','A ureter from each kidney carries urine to the urinary bladder.','The bladder stores urine; its muscular wall and sphincters participate in emptying.','The urethra carries urine from the bladder to the exterior during micturition.'],'Kidneys form urine, ureters transport it, the bladder stores it, and the urethra conducts it out of the body.'),
solved('iii','Describe the challenges and osmoregulatory adaptations of osmoconformers and osmoregulators in marine environment.',['Seawater presents a high external solute concentration. The effect on an animal depends on the concentration of its body fluids.','Osmoconformers maintain an overall body-fluid concentration close to the surrounding water, limiting net osmotic water movement.','Marine bony fishes are hypotonic to seawater and tend to lose water while gaining salts.','They drink water and remove excess ions through gill transport and kidney excretion.','The chapter contrasts cartilaginous fishes with bony fishes and describes approximately isotonic plasma in the former; it also describes salt-secreting rectal glands in some fishes.'],'Marine animals either reduce overall osmotic differences or actively regulate water and salts; the appropriate adaptations depend on their body-fluid concentration relative to seawater.'),
solved('iv','Describe the role of kidney as osmoregulatory organ.',['Kidneys filter plasma and selectively return water and useful solutes to blood.','The loops of Henle and medullary gradient allow urine to be concentrated.','During water shortage, ADH increases water reabsorption in the distal nephron and collecting ducts, producing a smaller volume of more concentrated urine.','With abundant water and reduced ADH, a larger volume of dilute urine is released.','Aldosterone increases sodium reabsorption through transport processes in the distal nephron.','By adjusting water and electrolyte excretion, kidneys help stabilize the composition of internal fluids.'],'Kidneys maintain water and salt balance by controlled filtration, selective reabsorption and secretion under hormonal regulation.'),
solved('v','What is renal failure? Describe its types and causes.',['Define renal failure as impairment of the kidneys’ normal waste-removal and regulatory functions.','Acute kidney failure develops rapidly, over hours or days. The chapter lists impaired blood flow, damaging substances and certain medicines among causes.','Chronic kidney failure develops gradually over years and can progress to end-stage disease.','The major chronic causes listed are diabetes and hypertension; long-term exposure to some medicines is also mentioned.','Distinguish the time course: sudden loss in acute failure versus progressive long-term loss in chronic failure.'],'Renal failure is loss of effective kidney function. Acute failure is rapid; chronic failure develops progressively, commonly in association with diabetes or hypertension.'),
solved('vi','Explain the procedure of peritoneal and hemodialysis.',['Dialysis removes wastes and excess water when kidney function is inadequate.','In peritoneal dialysis, dialysis fluid enters the abdominal cavity through a catheter and the peritoneal membrane separates it from blood in nearby vessels.','Solutes move across the membrane; after the dwell period, the used fluid is drained and replaced.','In haemodialysis, blood passes through tubing to an external dialyzer with a semipermeable membrane.','Exchange with dialysis fluid removes wastes and excess water, and the treated blood returns to the body.','The main comparison is the membrane used: the body’s peritoneum versus an artificial membrane in a dialyzer.'],'Peritoneal dialysis exchanges substances across the abdominal lining; haemodialysis circulates blood through an external membrane filter. These are descriptions of clinical procedures.'),
solved('vii','Describe various type of kidney stones and their causes.',['Calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate stones are the commonest types listed in the chapter. Increased urinary calcium or oxalate can favour their formation.','Uric-acid stones involve uric acid; the chapter relates raised levels to gout and high intake of meat protein.','Cystine stones contain the amino acid cystine.','Struvite or infection stones are associated with urinary-tract infection.','Reduced urine volume and excess stone-forming substances favour crystallization.','The chapter also discusses hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, hyperparathyroidism, hyperoxaluria and hyperuricemia as relevant conditions.'],'Major types are calcium stones, uric-acid stones, cystine stones and infection-related struvite stones; concentrated urine and excess stone-forming solutes favour their development.'),
]
ACTIVITIES=[dict(title='Science, Technology, and Society Connections',desc='<p>Describe the importance of kidney donation for the benefit of kidney failure patients.</p><p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> A suitable donated kidney can restore filtration and other kidney functions in a patient with end-stage kidney failure and may free the recipient from dialysis. Donor–recipient compatibility, transplantation care and control of rejection are important. Donation connects biological knowledge with an opportunity to improve another person’s health and daily life.</p>',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=57)],solutionOrigin='SpaceBook learning answer')]
ACTIVITIES.extend([
dict(title='Name the important kidney transplant centers in your province.',desc='<p><strong>Example answer for Khyber Pakhtunkhwa:</strong> Institute of Kidney Diseases (IKD), Hayatabad, Peshawar; and Rehman Medical Institute (RMI), Hayatabad, Peshawar.</p><p>These examples fit the province of this textbook; use the relevant province for your own response.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://ikdpeshawar.gkp.pk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Institute of Kidney Diseases</a>; <a href="https://int.rmi.edu.pk/about-rmi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rehman Medical Institute</a>.</p>',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=58)],solutionOrigin='SpaceBook regional example; official institution sources accessed 8 October 2026'),
dict(title='Draw a flow chart to show negative feedback of homeostasis mechanisms by taking an example of hormone.',desc='<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Water shortage → hypothalamus detects the change → posterior pituitary releases more ADH → distal nephron and collecting ducts reabsorb more water → blood water balance moves back toward normal → ADH release decreases.</p><p>The final response reduces the original disturbance, making this negative feedback.</p>',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=31)]),
dict(title='List some of the behavioral responses of the animals to maintain homeostasis.',desc='<p><strong>Example answer:</strong> Desert animals avoid daytime heat and become active at night. Animals can also move toward shade when hot or seek a warmer place when cold. Each action changes exposure to environmental conditions and helps maintain internal balance.</p>',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=34)]),
dict(title='Hypothesize kidney stones by studying urine reports of relevant patients.',desc='<p><strong>SpaceBook activity guide:</strong> Use the report supplied for the classroom activity. Record urine volume or concentration and any listed crystals or relevant solutes, then explain whether those observations support a stone-formation hypothesis. No patient report is supplied on this page, so no particular result can be inferred. A classroom hypothesis is not a diagnosis.</p>',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=49)])
])
EXERCISE=dict(mcqs=MCQS,shortQuestions=SHORT,longQuestions=LONG,activities=ACTIVITIES,sourceStatus='printed questions transcribed; learning answers supplied; full exercise translations pending',sourceReferences=[dict(printedPage=n) for n in [55,56,57,58]])
